  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through next Thursday)\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nThe ridge remains centered over the Desert Southwest this weekend,\nplacing South Central Texas beneath northwesterly flow aloft.\nMeanwhile, an upper level trough translating across the Plains is\nexpected to send a weak frontal boundary southward on Saturday and\nSunday. Ahead of this boundary, precipitable water values are\nexpected to increase, but guidance continues to range from around\n1.5 inches to near 2 inches. Isolated to scattered showers and\nstorms may develop as early as Saturday morning across portions of\nthe Hill Country, but most likely Saturday afternoon, as the \nfrontal boundary pushes south, and Saturday night into Sunday\nacross the Coastal Plains. A few strong storms cannot be ruled \nout, primarily with a gusty wind or small hail threat. Otherwise, \nrainfall amounts are forecast to remain fairly light as the front \nmoves through the region.The first week of August will feature \nseasonably hot temperatures and rain-free conditions as the \ninfluence of the ridge returns once again. Triple-digit heat index\nvalues are likely along and east of the I-35 corridor, so \ncontinue to practice heat safety!",
